INSERT INTO kb_articles (series, folder_id, slug, title, hook, solution_md, screenshots, read_minutes, published, published_at, sort_order)
SELECT 'Guide', f.id, $kb274$guide-automations$kb274$, $kb275$Automations — repetitive work, done for you$kb275$, $kb276$Start from a ready recipe — a birthday wish, a welcome email, a task when a SIP bounces — and switch it on.$kb276$, $kb277$Every event in the CRM — a rejected SIP, a redemption, a birthday — can start an automation: a checked sequence of messages and tasks.

![Automations](shot:1)

## Start an automation

1. Open **Settings → Automations** and click **New automation**.
2. Pick a recipe: **Birthday wish** (email every active client a birthday greeting on the day), **Welcome email** (welcome each new client as soon as they are added), **SIP bounce follow-up** (give the client's RM a high-priority task to recover it) or **Review reminder** (a task for the RM on each client's next review date). **See all recipes** opens the full library, and **Build from scratch** starts empty.
3. A recipe is added **switched off**, so you can check the message before anything is sent. Review the steps, choose the template for each message and switch it on.

![New automation](shot:2)

## Tabs

- **Rules** — your automations.
- **Actions** — the steps available and their settings.
- **Run history** — every time an automation ran, with its outcome.

## Safe by design

Every message an automation sends goes through the same pipeline as a hand-sent one: the client's consent, the compliance checks and the template approval. A message that is blocked is logged with the reason. In-flight runs finish on the version they started with, so editing an automation never breaks a running one.$kb277$, $kb278$[{"storageKey":"/kb/automations.jpg","caption":"Automations: Rules, Actions and Run history tabs.","alt":"Automations: Rules, Actions and Run history tabs."},{"storageKey":"/kb/automation-new.jpg","caption":"New automation: choose a recipe or start from scratch.","alt":"New automation: choose a recipe or start from scratch."}]$kb278$::jsonb, 4, true, now(), 37
  FROM kb_folders f WHERE lower(f.slug) = $kb279$guide-automation$kb279$
ON CONFLICT (lower(slug)) DO UPDATE
   SET folder_id = EXCLUDED.folder_id, title = EXCLUDED.title, hook = EXCLUDED.hook, solution_md = EXCLUDED.solution_md,
       screenshots = EXCLUDED.screenshots, read_minutes = EXCLUDED.read_minutes, published = true,
       published_at = now(), sort_order = EXCLUDED.sort_order, updated_at = now()
 WHERE kb_articles.series = 'Guide' AND kb_articles.published = false AND kb_articles.screenshots = '[]'::jsonb;

INSERT INTO kb_articles (series, folder_id, slug, title, hook, solution_md, screenshots, read_minutes, published, published_at, sort_order)
SELECT 'Guide', f.id, $kb280$guide-templates$kb280$, $kb281$Message templates — WhatsApp, email and SMS$kb281$, $kb282$Design, approve and reuse templates with merge fields, the firm's logo and each RM's sign-off.$kb282$, $kb283$Templates are the approved wording every message is built from.

![Message Templates](shot:1)

## Browse

1. Open **Settings → Message templates**.
2. Filter by channel (**All**, **WhatsApp**, **Email**, **SMS**) and by status (**Draft**, **Pending**, **Approved**, **Rejected**), or search by name or key.
3. Each row shows the channel, the category (utility or marketing) and when it was updated.

## Create a template

1. Click **New template**. A three-step wizard opens: **Set up**, **Design**, **Review & send**.
2. In step 1 choose how to reach the client — **Email** (a designed email with your logo and branding), **WhatsApp** or **SMS** (DLT-registered).
3. Give the **Template name**, say what it is for, and choose the **Message type** (for example Transactional: order, SIP and payment confirmations, statements) and the **Language**.
4. Pick a **starting point** — a ready-made template such as Welcome / Onboarding, SIP Confirmation or Lead — Thank You (First Touch) — or start blank. Click **Next: Design**, or **Save draft** to come back later.
5. In the designer, use merge fields such as the client's first name or the RM's name. The logo and each RM's sign-off blocks fill in from the firm's and the RM's own details.
6. WhatsApp templates are sent to Meta for approval; use **Fetch from WhatsApp** to bring approved templates in.

![New template](shot:2)

## Good to know

- Only approved templates can be sent.
- Merge values are escaped, so put plain text, not markup, in a field.
- Client-facing links use per-client tokens, never the firm's own login page.
- The firm's introduction (Settings → Email introduction) provides the "about us" fields for the new-lead welcome email.$kb283$, $kb284$[{"storageKey":"/kb/templates.jpg","caption":"Message Templates by channel and approval status.","alt":"Message Templates by channel and approval status."},{"storageKey":"/kb/template-new.jpg","caption":"New template: name, channel, category and body.","alt":"New template: name, channel, category and body."}]$kb284$::jsonb, 4, true, now(), 38
  FROM kb_folders f WHERE lower(f.slug) = $kb285$guide-automation$kb285$
ON CONFLICT (lower(slug)) DO UPDATE
   SET folder_id = EXCLUDED.folder_id, title = EXCLUDED.title, hook = EXCLUDED.hook, solution_md = EXCLUDED.solution_md,
       screenshots = EXCLUDED.screenshots, read_minutes = EXCLUDED.read_minutes, published = true,
       published_at = now(), sort_order = EXCLUDED.sort_order, updated_at = now()
 WHERE kb_articles.series = 'Guide' AND kb_articles.published = false AND kb_articles.screenshots = '[]'::jsonb;

